MOFs have emerged as novel materials with considerable promise in catalysis.11–20 Regarding their use as heterogeneous catalysts, MOFs offer at least three different possibilities derived from their porous structure. Perhaps the most widely explored strategy has been to take advantage of exchangeable coordination positions around the metal ions not compromised with the lattice that will act as active sites. Also, recently the importance of structural defects around the metal nodes and their implication in catalysis has been revealed.21
A second methodology is based on the use of the ligands as active sites.22 Since terephthalates and benzene tricarboxylates are among the most commonly used ligands, the synthesis of modified MOFs in which besides the parent aromatic carboxylates is performed with some proportion or with total replacement of the aromatic carboxylate by some derivatives containing additional acidic or basic groups introduce active sites derived from the nature of the organic linker substituents. This approach has been widely used replacing terephthalate with aminoterephthalate particularly in the case of UiO-66 MOFs.23
The third approach that derives from the large pore volume available in MOFs consists of the incorporation of guest species in the internal void space that will act as a catalytic site.19 A recent precedent has reviewed various methodologies to encapsulate organic and inorganic guests inside MOFs using the ship-in-a-bottle or bottle-around-the-ship approaches, but it has not focused primarily on the catalytic activity of the resulting solids when they include MNPs.19 Fig. 2 illustrates the three different strategies that have been used to exploit MOFs in heterogeneous catalysis. In the present review, we will focus on describing the reports in which this third methodology has been used to develop heterogeneous catalysts comprising MNPs inside MOFs.
The following section will describe briefly the various methodologies employed for the preparation of MNPs inside MOFs, the precursors used for the various MNPs and the conditions and methods used to obtain MNPs inside MOFs. The various techniques used to characterize the MNPs embedded on MOFs are also discussed. Finally, the factors controlling the location of MNPs, the limitations to prepare MNPs exclusively inside the MOF matrix, the subsequent effect on catalysis and the merits of using MOFs as host material compared to other solid matrices are also discussed.
## 1.1 Methodologies
Loading of MNPs inside the porous matrices of MOFs is a promising field of research in catalysis that complements the extensive studies in which the catalytic activity of pure MOFs has been investigated.11–13,24 In general one could think of two different approaches for the synthesis of MNPs inside MOFs. The first and most widely used method is to use MOFs as stabilizing host material providing a confined space that limits particle growth and impede agglomeration. The procedure consists of the stepwise precursor infiltration, followed by decomposition or reduction. In this way, the dimension and shape of the NPs, synthesized directly in the pores of the framework, should be controlled by the pore size, shape and channel structure of the host material (Fig. 3). Also, it has been reported that simple grinding of MOFs with organometallic complexes could also lead to formation of NPs inside MOFs.25 It would be of interest to further exploit this methodology to determine which are the most suitable Au precursors, in which MOFs can be incorporated and the control on the particle size. Recently, the ability of MOF-5 to photocatalytically reduce silver upon UV irradiation has been reported.26 Stirring an ethanolic silver nitrate solution in the presence of MOF-5 under UV irradiation resulted in the formation of silver NPs inside MOF-5 crystallites.26 The generation of Ag NPs is evidenced by the color change observed from white powder to dark brown-black. The MOF-5 structure is preserved without crystallinity loss as shown by an X-ray diffraction pattern. Besides Ag, similar photocatalytic reduction could be probably applied to Au, Pt and other noble metals. The main advantage of the photochemical generation of MNPs can be spatial resolution that can be achieved by using a light beam to effect the reduction leading to metallic particles. Considering that the MOFs crystallite size can be even in the millimeter scale and the light beam can have submicrometric resolution, it is possible to imagine that by photochemical generation it should be possible to form MNPs in the core of MOF crystallites leaving the outer part of the crystallite without NPs. This photochemical procedure is, however, not general for all MOFs and derives from the behavior of MOF-5 as a semiconductor that is able to effect the photocatalytic reduction of noble metal ions. Thus, in contrast to the behavior of MOF-5, when MIL-53(Al), a MOF material containing the same terephthalate ligand but with ${\mathrm{Al}}^{3+}$ as a metal ion, was subjected to the same treatment, formation of Ag NPs does not occur. The reason for this is that MIL-53(Al) cannot be considered a semiconductor that is the necessary condition for this photocatalytic reduction.
The second methodology involves the protection in solution of preformed NPs stabilized by organic molecules, surfactants and polymers as capping agents and the subsequent addition of suitable precursors to construct the framework around preformed NPs (Fig. 4). As an example of this strategy, the synthesis of Au ${\bf N P s/M O F s}$ heterostructured nanocomposites by self-assembly of MOFs around suitably functionalized NPs has been reported recently.27 The method involves the preparation of Au NPs in the presence of 11-mercaptoundecanoic acid linked by ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions and preferential growth of $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}]_{n}$ frameworks by adding the organic linker namely benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (BTC) resulting in the formation of Au $\mathrm{NPs}/[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}]_{n}$ nanocomposites.27 It can be foreseen that this approach will also serve to construct other NPs/MOFs materials and more examples are surely welcomed to expand the scope and demonstrate the versatility of the technique.
On the other hand, microwave irradiation has also been used for simultaneous activation of the pores of MIL-101 and the rapid chemical reduction of metal precursors in the presence of a reducing agent to obtain MNPs supported on MIL-101.28 For example, small Pd, Cu and Pd–Cu nanoparticles of $2\mathrm{-}3~\mathrm{nm}$ within the pores and larger particles of $4{\div}6\ \mathrm{nm}$ on the surface of the MIL-101 crystals have been reported using this heating procedure.
Although, several approaches for embedding NPs into the cavities of zeolites, porous silicas and aluminas via immersion in colloidal solutions have been reported,29 this method finds difficulty with MOFs. It appears that this procedure requires MOFs with suitable cavity sizes to incorporate surfactant stabilized NPs whose hydrodynamic radii are typically larger than $3\mathrm{nm}$
## 1.2 Precursors and reduction methods
Suitable precursor molecules for the synthesis of embedded NPs may be chlorides or nitrates of the corresponding transition metal, as well as organometallic complexes often used as precursors to load NPs either by solid grinding or by infiltration.30 Depending on the properties of the included precursor, the decomposition conditions have to be selected in such a way that the host matrix is stable during the reduction procedure. Generally, metal ions exchanged in MOFs are reduced with molecular hydrogen, hydrazine or $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ to generate MNPs embedded inside MOFs. Attention should be paid to the loading time and hydrogen pressure since these parameters play a vital role in determining the size of the NPs. In some cases, ethanolic solution or methanol: $\operatorname{scCO}_{2}$ has also been used as a reducing agent to load NPs inside the MOF matrices.31
## 1.3 Location of NPs inside MOFs
The key point of using a porous matrix to embed MNPs is that in this way one can control the particle size distribution, limited by the dimensions of the cavity, avoid NPs agglomeration by immobilizing them permanently inside the cavities and still allowing the accessibility of the NPs to the substrates. However, in spite of the simplicity of the methodology consisting of encapsulation of NP inside porous matrices, experimental proof of the concept has many problems mostly derived from the difficulty of preparation of the NPs exclusively inside the cavities. In fact, one weakest point of this method is how to avoid the formation of NPs on the external surface. Experimental techniques to address the topological distributions of NPs either outside or inside MOF crystallites are generally providing indirect evidence based on the particle size (larger particles than the cavities are assumed to be outside) and variation of total surface area (a gradual decrease of surface area with loading suggests the internal location of the NPs). However, it may be found that particles larger than the crystallographic cavity dimensions are formed inside if two or more cavities are occupied by a single MNP or if partial destruction of the framework occurs in the process of NPs formation. On the other hand, small NPs can also be present on the external surface of MOF crystallites. Therefore, caution must be taken when accepting particle size distribution as evidence of the NPs location. In addition, the decrease of surface area never excludes the presence of NPs on the external surface of MOF crystallites. Moreover, MNPs can be preferentially located at the pore mouths blocking the access to the internal surface, leading to an apparent micropore volume reduction.
An additional comment is that for this type of studies in which heterogeneous catalysts based on MNPs inside MOFs are used, the crystal stability and topology of the MOF are key issues to be considered. The framework of the inorganic matrix should be robust enough to have some guarantees of the subsequent applicability of the system. Also, tri-directional MOFs in which the geometry of the pores defines larger cavities interconnected by smaller windows are the most appropriate for the purpose of MNPs immobilization. In these MOFs, the MNPs could be located on the cavity but could not diffuse outside due to size limitation to cross the windows. MOFs with a pore system formed by channels are less appropriate candidates to act as hosts because the NPs can freely move through the channel and eventually can meet other NPs and grow further or migrate to the external surface.
## 1.4 Merits of MOFs as hosts
The concept of using porous hosts to immobilize MNPs has been already largely exploited using zeolites, mesoporous aluminosilicates and other porous inorganic or organic materials.32–34 The rationale behind this methodology is to accomplish particle size stabilization by embedding MNPs into a rigid cavity of a limited dimension.33 In a first approach, it is considered that hosts such as zeolites or porous aluminosilicates do not establish interactions with the MNPs. In the case of MOFs, the situation is somewhat more complicated due to the nature of the nodal metal ion and organic linkers that should interact with the embedded MNP guest by coordination and p–p forces.35
Although, the concept of encapsulation of a guest to avoid its particle growth is appealing, this methodology raises issues about the diffusion of reagents and products through the host pore system. Thus, in some cases, the catalytic activities of the embedded NPs in aluminosilicates decrease considerably when included inside the matrix. The main reason for this is a decrease in the accessibility to the NPs and the restriction to the diffusion of the substrates. Also, in some cases, it has been reported that MNPs embedded on MCM-41 resulted in decrease in its activity when it was reused due to severe collapse of the pore structure of the MCM-41 support and agglomeration of NPs.36 The agglomeration could also be predicted based on their porous structures with straight channels and the lack of an adequate confinement effect. As a result, there is a possibility that MNPs escape out of the pores. For this reason, in order to improve the efficiency of this system, there is still need for novel porous hosts. Also, zeolites and microporous aluminosilicates are considered to be one of the ideal hosts to support MNPs.33,37 Recently a review has appeared delineating similarities and dissimilarities between zeolites and MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts for liquid phase reactions.24 The lack of flexibility in the synthesis and the limitation in the available pore sizes are the main drawbacks of zeolites as hosts compared to MOFs.
In this regard, MOFs offer several advantages derived from their three directional structures and further additional merits derived from organic linkers to stabilize MNPs and in some cases the robust nature and moderate thermal stability. Although, in many cases there is still more information needed to assess the robust nature of MOFs as host materials to embed MNPs, some MOFs containing NPs have shown for many organic reactions excellent activity due to the confinement effect and the limitation of the particles that remain constrained avoiding their growth before and after catalysis.
Many efforts have been made to synthesize aluminophosphates, zeolites and MCM-41 with the desired pore size and appropriate morphology.38–40 In contrast, a large variety of MOFs have been reported with micro to macro pore systems and it is relatively easy to obtain different crystal structures with a wide range of metal ions with various rigid organic linkers and with different pore systems. Hence, the choice of selecting the suitable host material depending on the size of the NPs required could be easily achieved.
## 1.5 Stability of MOFs
In the present review, we are going to comment on the use of NPs embedded inside MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts for liquid-phase organic reactions. As in any use of MOFs in catalysis, the key points to be addressed are their stability, activity and selectivity under the reaction conditions and the deactivation pathways.41 The stability of the MOFs used as host material must be checked after loading MNPs as there is a possibility to destroy the crystal structure during loading or in the reduction procedure. The crystal structure must be retained to enjoy the benefits of high surface area, pore volume and accessibility of their pore allowing the substrates and reagents to reach the active sites. Also, caution must be taken in the reduction procedure to maintain enough surface area without blocking the pores by the NPs or by unwanted reduction by-products when using organometallic precursors.
In addition to providing conversion and selectivity data, when using NPs/MOFs as catalysts, it is also necessary to determine the stability of the material and the lack of leaching of active MNPs to the solution. These points are typically addressed by checking the crystal structure, determining the surface area and TEM after the catalytic reactions as well as performing analysis of the solutions at final reaction time. Productivity, defined as the amount of product that can be obtained with a given amount of catalyst before deactivation, is also important data that must be considered in order to assess the feasibility of using MOFs as catalysts.
When dealing with NPs embedded or supported on any solid matrices, leaching or migration under operation conditions is a general problem that has to be carefully surveyed and in the case that the total absence of leaching is proposed it has to be convincingly proved. Also, MNPs can easily undergo agglomeration upon storage and evidence has to be presented showing that MNPs maintain their size before and after catalysis. Generally, the role of the support is to stabilize the MNPs impeding their growth.33 When confined in a restricted space, growth beyond the dimensions of the cavity cannot occur due to mechanical forces. In a simple way, inclusion of MNPs in porous hosts is a general methodology to stabilize particle dimensions without the need of claiming other type of interactions.19 It is clear however that this is a simplistic view and the host never plays exclusively a passive role merely defining the cavity dimensions.19 Since MOF has metal nodes that may have exchangeable coordination positions and the aromatic linkers, these units certainly will establish charge transfer interactions with MNPs besides mechanical stabilizations. Thus, it has been recently reported that organic linkers having uncoordinated carbonyl groups stabilize Pd NPs due to the interaction of this type of substituents with the MNPs.42 In the case of MOFs, one additional problem is the stability of the organic linker to the reduction procedures employed to form MNPs and the strength of the metal cluster–linker interaction that could be not strong enough to completely avoid metal leaching under the typical conditions and solvents used in organic reactions.
## 1.6 Characterization
The obtained NPs/MOFs must be suitably characterized before and after applying them in catalysis. Powder XRD of pure and NPs/MOFs must be compared to evidence that the crystal structure of the host has not changed after loading NPs. Nitrogen isothermal adsorption would give data about surface area and pore volume before and after loading of NPs. Porosity measurements would give valuable data about the available space for the reactants to diffuse to the active sites. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy reports on the oxidation state of the loaded NPs after the reduction process. TEM gives the information about the particle size of loaded NPs but care must be taken to avoid damage of the host matrix as the high energy electron beam supplied by the instrument can lead to the destruction of MOF permitting the formation of higher particles as artifacts. This will be discussed in the later section of this review. In any case, it is also important to check the particle size and the oxidation state of the NPs after catalysis as some of the particles may be relocated outside the MOF matrix or undergo some modification.
## 1.7 Heterogeneous catalysis by MOFs
In recent years, MOFs received much attention among the porous materials as heterogeneous catalysts due to their striking features compared to silicas or zeolite materials24 motivated by the flexible synthesis of large varieties of MOFs with different metal ion or organic linkers.3 The list of reactions catalyzed by MOFs as solid Lewis acid includes acetalization or ketalization of carbonyl compounds,43–45 rearrangement and ring opening of epoxides,46–50 methylation of amines,51 condensation,52–56 cyanosilylation reactions57,58 and heterocycle synthesis.59–62 In addition, MOFs have also been used as heterogeneous oxidation catalysts63–76 and recently a detailed review has appeared in this topic.12 Other catalytic reactions catalyzed by MOFs include hydrogenation77 and base catalysis.23,78–80 Apart from the use of MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts, other applications include gas storage,81–83 biomedicine,84 chemical sensors,85 gas separations,86 as thin films,87,88 luminescent,89 and asymmetric catalysts.90
For most of the previous reactions mentioned, industrial processes in fine chemicals are still using generally homogeneous catalysts.91 There is a clear tendency to convert a homogeneous process into heterogeneous reactions.92 For this purpose, insoluble organic polymers, and in particular polystyrenes,93 active carbons94 and inorganic oxides95 are the preferred supports. Although, the use of MOFs for industrial processes is still far from real applications, they offer many opportunities and it is likely that the research on this field at the laboratory scale will trigger the interest for scaling up their use and over the time they can reach industrial processes.96
The next sections of this review will describe the catalytic activity of MNPs embedded in MOFs depending on the metal.
## 2. Pd NPs/MOFs
Palladium catalysts beyond hydrogenations and oxidations and particularly the use of Pd NPs for C–C bond formation have become one of the major achievements in chemistry in the last century.123,124 Many of these processes are catalyzed by Pd NPs of small size.124 At the industrial scale, active carbon has been the preferred support for Pd NPs using materials similar to those employed as catalysts for hydrogenations. A review on the use of $\mathrm{Pd}$ as heterogeneous catalysts for C–C coupling can be found in the literature.125
Stabilization of the NPs dimensions can be achieved by encapsulation of the MNPs inside a porous host such as MOF that impedes NP agglomeration and growth. In this regard, and following the extensive work in which Pd NPs have been incorporated into other porous solids,19 it is of interest to establish properties of Pd NPs embedded in MOFs. This section is the core of the present review due to the large number of reports dealing with the preparation of Pd NPs entrapped inside MOFs for their use as heterogeneous catalysts compared to other MNPs.
Pd NPs embedded in MIL-100(Al) [Al(III)BTC] were successfully synthesized by a chemical wetting method using ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{PdCl}}_{4}$ as a precursor, followed by reduction with hydrogen to obtain $\mathrm{Pd/MIL\mathrm{-}100(A l)}$ with $10\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ loading of Pd.126 During the Pd loading process, the crystal structure was unaltered as evidenced by powder XRD. Highly dispersed Pd NPs with mean size $2.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ are presumed to be located within the pores of MIL-100(Al). This changes the textural properties with a loss of specific surface area and micropore volume from 1200 to $380~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and 0.65 to $0.33\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for MIL-100(Al) and Pd/MIL-100 (Al), respectively. The increase of hydrogen storage capacity at low temperature can be correlated with the decrease of the specific surface area and pore volume after Pd impregnation. At ambient temperature, the hydrogen uptake in the composite is almost twice that of the pristine material. This can be attributed to the formation of Pd hydride, as clearly proven by in situ XRD. This study clearly exemplifies the effect of loading MNPs inside the porous matrices to fine tune the particle size of resulting NPs/MOFs.
This section will illustrate the catalytic activity obtained with Pd NPs embedded on different MOFs in various organic reactions such as condensation, $\mathrm{C-}C$ coupling, hydrogenation and indole synthesis among others.
## 2.1 Condensation
Chromium(III) terephthalate or MIL- $101^{127}$ has been probably the most widely used support for deposition of NPs including Pd due to its high chemical and thermal stability compared to other MOFs. Particularly, this MOF has shown good stability in water and hence proved to be a water tolerant catalyst. This water stability is important considering that other MOFs undergo extensive structural damage under these conditions. MIL-101 has two types of zeotypic mesopores with free diameters of ca. 29 and $34\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ accessible through two microporous windows of ca. 12 and $16\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ . MIL-101 has very large BET (Brunauer–Emmett–Teller) and Langmuir surface areas $(4100\pm200~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $5900\pm300\mathrm{{m}^{2}\mathrm{{g}^{-1}}}$ , respectively) and numerous potential unsaturated chromium sites (up to $3.0\ \mathrm{mmol\g^{-1}})$ .
One important point that has been rarely addressed is how to favor the preferential formation of MNPs inside MOFs. Unless special protocols are employed, formation of the MNPs must also occur outside the pores this leading to particles of size larger than the pore dimensions. Ferey and coworkers have used organic amines as grafting agents to drive the preferential loading of palladium NPs inside the pores of MIL-101.106 3-Aminopropylsilane (APS) and ethylenediamine (ED) were grafted on the Lewis acid sites of MIL-101 and, then, used the resulting modified solid as a host for further loading of palladium MNPs (Fig. 5) and as a basic catalyst for the Knoevenagel condensation.
Amine-grafted on MIL-101 was used as catalyst for Knoevenagel condensation of benzaldehyde with ethyl cyanoacetate at $353\mathrm{~K~}$ (Scheme 1). ED-MIL-101 showed $97.7\%$ conversion with $99.1\%$ selectivity while APS-MIL-101 showed $96.3\%$ conversion with $99.3\%$ selectivity.106 In contrast to the amino modified MOF, MIL-101 showed $31.5\%$ conversion with the same high selectivity as that of amine-grafted MIL-101. As indicated previously, amine-grafted MIL-101 was also used as a host to include preferentially Pd NPs into the internal pores according to the procedure shown in Fig. 5. During the loading of Pd NPs, the crystal structure of MIL-101 was retained with slight variations in the intensity of Bragg peaks.
TEM images of $1\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ Pd metal-containing MIL-101 materials $(0.93\mathrm{-}0.96~\mathrm{wt\%}$ based on inductively coupled plasma analysis) firmly support the successful encapsulation of Pd NPs inside the solid mesopores since the size of Pd NPs is $2{\-}4\mathrm{nm}$ which is well in agreement with the cage diameters. However in spite of this special procedure some NPs are still formed outside the pores with particle size greater than $20~\mathrm{nm}$ and, therefore, a simple procedure for the selective formation of Pd NPs inside the pores is still to be developed. It would be important to determine the amount of internal Pd in the functionalized MIL-101 with respect to the as-prepared MIL-101 material.
Palladium loaded APS-MIL-101 and ED-MIL-101 have shown comparable activity to that of $1\ \mathrm{wt\%}\ \mathrm{Pd/C}$ as catalyst for the Heck coupling between iodobenzene and acrylic acid in $N,N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -dimethylacetamide as solvent at $393\mathrm{~K~}$ (Scheme 1). The recyclability test confirmed the absence of Pd leaching.106 Considering that iodobenzene is the most reactive aryl halide for $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ cross coupling reactions and in order to check the catalytic activity of Pd NPs loaded on amino modified MIL-101, it would be of interest to explore the scope of this Pd catalyst for other more demanding couplings and expand the use of this heterogeneous catalyst for other palladium catalyzed reactions. One intriguing observation was that the temporal profile of the reaction using the Pd amine-grafted MIL-101 as catalyst exhibits a short induction period of 0.5 to $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . This induction period has been interpreted as indicating the slow diffusion of reactants to reach the active sites. However, considering the small reactant size this hypothesis of diffusion limitation should have been supported by additional tests including the use of larger substrates, catalyst with smaller crystallite sizes, etc.
Pd NPs have also been loaded in MIL-101 by incipient wetness impregnation of palladium nitrate as a precursor without prior modification of the MOF. A study of the influence of Pd loading on the catalytic activity has established that Pd/MIL-101 of different Pd loading exhibits different behavior in terms of conversion and selectivity for the liquid phase condensation of acetone under hydrogen to methyl isobutyl ketone (MIBK, Scheme 2).107 The reason for this could be the different particle size distribution and location as the Pd loading varies. In general, higher loading makes the formation of external large Pd particles unavoidable and low loadings are advisable if embedded, small NPs with narrow diameter distributions are desired. For instance, $0.11~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Pd showed NP mean diameters of $2.5\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which are in good agreement with the pore diameters of MIL-101. However, for a loading of $0.87\mathrm{\wt\%}$ of $\mathrm{Pd}$ , the proportion of small PD NPs is low and most of the Pd was aggregated in various degrees, resulting in much larger Pd particles with mean diameter increasing to $4.2\ \pm\ 1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , indicating that they must be located mostly at the external surface of the crystallites.
In the absence of palladium, MIL-101 showed the highest selectivity to mesityl oxide (MO, $74.9\%$ ) while the addition of Pd over MIL-101 increased the conversion of acetone and selectivity to MIBK, respectively. On the other hand, increasing the palladium loading on the MIL-101 support increases the conversion of acetone with decreasing selectivity of MIBK and increasing selectivity of isopropanol (IP).107 This variation in the product distribution indicates that acetone is preferentially hydrogenated to isopropanol by larger Pd NPs. It is relevant to note that the formation of MIBK requires the simultaneous presence of acid and metal sites, while the formation of IP requires only metal sites. Thus, for the $0.11/0.87~\mathrm{wt\%}$ loadings, $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ exhibited $73.5/70.6\%$ conversion with $65.5/46.8\%$ and $6.6/15.8\%$ selectivity of MIBK and IP, respectively, at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $0.75\mathrm{MPa}$ hydrogen pressure under liquid phase reaction conditions.107 The best performance of $0.11~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Pd/MIL-101 at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ was determined at $60\%$ conversion giving $90.2\%$ selectivity towards MIBK. The Pd loaded on the MIL-101 catalyst $(0.11~\mathrm{wt\%})$ ) could be reused five times without any changes in the crystallinity of MIL-101. Further, TEM studies revealed that the particle size remained unaltered during the course of the reaction and AAS showed no leaching of palladium. There is no doubt that this tandem reaction (aldolic condensation followed by $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{C}}$ hydrogenation) in addition of being of large industrial relevance constitutes an excellent test reaction that by analysis of the product distribution can report on the relative activity of the acid and metal sites. These catalytic activity data nicely illustrate that the product selectivity can be associated to the internal location of the MNPs and, therefore, to the metal loading.
## 2.2 Hydrogenation
Catalytic hydrogenation is one of the most important reactions extensively employed in industry. Traditionally selective hydrogenations are catalyzed by Ni, Pd, Ru, $\mathrm{Pt}^{128-131}$ and $\mathrm{Au}^{132,133}$ mainly stabilized on active carbons.
MOF-5, one of the first MOFs being described, has attracted considerable attention and has been used as a support to load palladium. Different strategies to reduce adsorbed $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ forming NPs have been tested with the aim of identifying a suitable procedure to obtain an optimal catalyst.98 In this context, $1\mathrm{wt\%}$ of palladium was loaded with $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ by incipient wetness and, then, reduced under vacuum at 150 or $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or reduced under hydrogen flow at 150 or $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Impregnation of evacuated MOF with a Pd salt followed by hydrogenation is a simple straightforward method to obtain Pd loaded MOFs.
Using hydrogenation of styrene (Scheme 3) as model reaction, it was observed that $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ reduced at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ or $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum produced less than $85\%$ ethylbenzene after $8\mathrm{~h~}$ , while $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ reduced with a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ flow at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ showed after $8\mathrm{~h~}$ a concentration of ethylbenzene of $97\%$ and $94\%$ , respectively. Interestingly, all the four $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ showed reactivity for 1-octene hydrogenation with less than $0.2\%$ of 1-octene remaining after $9\mathrm{{h}}$ .98 On the other hand, Pd/MOF-5 showed only $30\%$ conversion of cyclooctene to cyclooctane after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . This may be due to the large dimension of the cyclooctene compared to styrene or 1-octene or to the difference in activation energies depending on the substrate. No leaching of palladium was observed during the course of the reaction but, however, minor changes in the XRD patterns were observed. This is a remarkable fact considering the poor structural stability of MOF-5.
One point of large interest is not only to determine the catalytic activity of Pd loaded MOFs, but also to assess that these samples exhibit as solid catalysts enhanced performance compared to related, commonly used supported catalysts. In this regard, it was found that $1\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Pd/MOF-5 (prepared by reduction of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ under hydrogen flow at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ showed higher catalytic activity $94\%$ ethylbenzene) in styrene hydrogenation after $8\mathrm{~h~}$ as compared with $1\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd/Norit A (Norit A is a microporous carbon; $76\%$ ethylbenzene) and $1\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ $54\%$ ethylbenzene).98 However, the low chemical stability of MOF-5 as a host and its sensitivity against moisture leaves still open the door for new MOF systems as host matrices to develop heterogeneous catalysts for the hydrogenation reactions with better performance than current Pd catalysts.
One of these attempts to increase the structural stability of the catalyst has reported the preparation of Pd NPs embedded on MIL-101 obtained by the incipient wetness method using ${\mathrm{Pd}}(\mathrm{acac})_{2}$ solutions.98 This precursor has the advantage that during the reduction step a neutral innocuous acetylacetone molecule is formed. In contrast inorganic $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ salts will form strongly acidic inorganic compounds that can produce framework damage. The resulting $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ has Pd NPs of a mean diameter of $1.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ .98 This $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ sample was tested for the selective hydrogenation of styrene and ethylene in the presence of acetylene using hydrogen.105 The use of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL}{\cdot}101\$ as a hydrogenation catalyst leads to a complete conversion of styrene to ethylbenzene after $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ . Under the same reaction conditions, Pd/MOF-5 yields for the same reaction time were $80\%$ of the hydrogenation product, whereas Pd/Norit A affords $65\%$ and $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ less than $50\%$ ethylbenzene. Moreover, $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ exhibited also the highest activity for the hydrogenation of cis-cyclooctene compared to the other palladium supported catalysts.105 The higher activity of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ as compared to $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ may be due to the larger pore size of the MIL-101 matrix. This explanation exemplifies the potential advantages derived from the efficient integration of active sites inside and not outside105 the pore system of adequate void dimension.
A different preparation method based on coprecipitation of zinc nitrate hexahydrate, 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid and palladium(II) nitrate dihydrate as precursors has been applied successfully to obtain a $0.5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ palladium embedded on MOF-5.99 The specific activity of palladium embedded on MOF-5 for the hydrogenation of ethyl-cinnamate by molecular hydrogen was twice as high as that of a commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ catalyst with the same Pd content. No leaching of palladium was observed during hydrogenation. In repeated runs the palladium embedded on MOF-5 did not show any loss in activity. However a complete loss of micropore volume was observed indicating the complete collapse of the crystal structure. These adsorption data would indicate that the palladium is not located inside the micropores but probably deposited on the outer surface of the crystals,99 but additional characterization data are needed to explain the catalytic activity in the absence of porosity.
Palladium NPs of different mean particle size have been synthesized in the host structure of MIL-101 by chemical vapor deposition with $[(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{5}{-}\mathbf{C}_{5}\mathbf{H}_{5})\mathbf{P}\mathbf{d}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{3}{-}\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{5})]$ as the Pd precursor complex.108 Reduction of the Pd precursor complex with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ under mild conditions gave rise to Pd NPs inside the pores of MIL-101.108 The mildness of the reduction conditions, especially the temperature, and the formation of volatile organic ligands allow the control of particle size. For example, reduction at room temperature gave rise to cavity-sizeconform Pd particles with a mean size of about $2.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ . When the reduction was performed at elevated temperature $(70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , smaller Pd NPs with a mean particle size of around $1.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ were obtained.
These Pd/MIL-101 catalysts were used for the reduction of aryl alkyl ketones to the corresponding alcohols and/or arylalkane by molecular hydrogen.108 In this study, acetophenone and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -dimethylpropiophenone were selected as probe molecules to address the influence of molecular dimensions on the catalytic activity. In general, the catalyst with smaller particle size showed good performance for small substrates $(>95\%$ conversion) at any reaction temperature in the range studied, while for large substrates the high conversion was achieved only at $80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . In contrast, the catalyst with larger Pd particle size showed high conversion regardless of the dimension of the substrate either small or big $(>95\%$ conversion) at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The temperature plays a strong influence and at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ only the smaller substrates were reduced with $>99\%$ conversion while the bigger substrates exhibited much lower conversion around $40\%$ . None of the catalysts was active for the reduction of aliphatic ketones. The catalyst showed good stability and even after the 11th run, no palladium leaching was detected in the liquid phase.108
Recently, Pd NPs embedded on MIL- $101^{109}$ were tested as bifunctional, water-compatible, heterogeneous catalysts for the hydrogenation of phenols in aqueous medium using hydrogen (Scheme 4). The Pd loadings were 1, 3, 4, 5 and $6\mathrm{wt\%}$ on MIL-101 and the particle size ranges from $2\mathrm{-}3~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . At $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $5\%$ $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ showed a phenol conversion of around $99.9\%$ with $>99.9\%$ selectivity towards cyclohexanone in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ with $0.1~\mathrm{\MPa}$ hydrogen pressure. Increasing the temperature $(40\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and pressure (1 MPa) accelerated the reaction rate by reducing the time from 7 and $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , respectively, to achieve $99.9\%$ conversion and selectivity. The catalyst was reused five times without any loss in their activity or Pd leaching. In contrast, hydrogenation of substituted phenols led to the formation of some percentage (around $5\%$ ) of the corresponding substituted cyclohexanols.
## 2.3 Coupling reactions
Palladium NPs embedded on MIL- $101^{110}$ prepared by impregnation of palladium nitrate as a precursor at a loading of around $1\%$ do not deteriorate the crystallinity of MIL-101 as evidenced by XRD. Pd NPs were highly dispersed on the matrix with a small mean diameter of $1.9\pm0.7\mathrm{nm}$ as revealed by TEM images.110 For $\mathrm{C-}C$ cross-coupling reactions, it is well known that the activation $\mathrm{\bfC}\mathrm{-}{\bf C}\mathrm{1}$ bond is much more difficult than $\mathbf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{Br}$ and $\mathrm{C-I}$ bonds and, in general, it requires harsh reaction conditions.134,135 Moreover, the presence of electron donor substituents on the aryl ring of the aromatic halide makes also these substrates less reactive for $\mathrm{C-C}$ coupling. Thus, if the catalytic activity of a solid Pd catalyst wants to be really evaluated, it should be employed for highly demanding substrates such as 4-chloroanisole.
In this context, Pd/MIL-101 has been used as a heterogeneous catalyst for the Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reaction between 4-chloroanisole and phenylboronic acid in aqueous media.110 It is relevant to mention that MIL-101 as a support showed high stability both in aqueous media and in other organic solvents.136 This $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ catalyst showed better yield of 4-phenylanisole $(82\%)$ when the reaction was performed with tetrabutylammonium bromide using sodium methoxide as base in $6\mathrm{~h~}$ . On the other hand, $1\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ exhibited only $35\%$ of the cross coupling product. This $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ catalyst showed high activity to promote the coupling of chlorobenzene as well as electron-donor and electron-withdrawing substituted aryl chlorides, affording the corresponding biphenyl compounds in excellent yields under the optimized reaction conditions (Scheme 5).
It should be noted that even 2-chloroanisole, which is both deactivated for the Suzuki coupling and sterically hindered, could also be coupled in good yield $(81\%)$ . Interestingly, the unreduced $\mathrm{Pd^{\mathrm{II}}/M I L{-}l01}$ afforded essentially no $\mathrm{C-}\mathrm{C}$ bond formation indicating that palladium in the reduced form is the active site for the Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reaction. The same catalyst was further tested for Ullmann self-coupling of 4-chloroanisole and observed $97\%$ yield of $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dimethoxybiphenyl under a nitrogen atmosphere and $96\%$ in air (Scheme 5).110
The catalyst was reused five times for the Ullmann coupling and XRD revealed that the crystallinity was retained after extensive use. Less than $0.2\%$ of the total palladium present in the solid was observed in the solution as determined by AAS analyses, while TEM images showed that the particle size during the coupling reaction was maintained $2\pm\:0.6\:\:\mathrm{nm}$ . Overall, these catalytic data show an excellent catalytic activity for $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ that compares with the most efficient solid catalysts ever reported. Pd/MIL-101 is able to promote $\mathrm{C-}C$ coupling of aryl chlorides having methoxy substituents and even ortho substituted that are really less reactive due to the steric encumbrance of the resulting biphenyls.
In a way to support palladium NPs, a different strategy was adopted using amino functionalized MIL-53(Al) $\mathrm{\small{[Al(OH)(H_{2}N{-}B D C)]}}$ $\begin{array}{r l r}{(\mathbf{N}\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{-B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C}}&{{}=}&{2\mathbf{-}}\end{array}$ aminoterephthalic acid) MOF as a support and introducing Pd by a direct ionic exchange followed by reduction (Fig. 6).103 This strategy resembles the work carried out by Ferey and coworkers modifying $\mathbf{Cr-MIL-}101$ with amino groups to achieve the preferential adsorption of $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ into the micropore void of the MOF. However, the present strategy is simpler in the sense that the MOF interior has been designed to already contain an amino ligand for the optimal interaction with $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ .
A $:0.97\mathrm{wt\%}$ loading, the palladium NPs were highly dispersed in MIL-53(Al)– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ with a mean diameter of $3.12~\mathrm{nm}$ .
This catalyst was tested for Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reaction between aryl halides and phenylboronic acid.103 Good yield of biphenyl $(>90\%)$ was obtained when iodo and bromobenzene were used as substrates but, however, only trace amounts were observed when chlorobenzene was the reagent. Addition of sodium methoxide resulted in the formation of the cross-coupling product in $59\%$ yield, but deterioration of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was observed. Also, this catalyst failed to give any product when one of the reactants was treated alone under identical conditions. In contrast, Pd/MIL-53(Al) and $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ resulted in $45\%$ and $37\%$ yield, respectively, which indicates that MIL-53(Al)– $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ is more selective towards cross coupling than the other Pd catalysts. Encouraging yields were obtained using $\mathrm{Pd/MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ as catalyst both with electron donating or withdrawing substituents on the aryl group of aryl halide or phenylboronic acid. In addition, sterically hindered 2-methoxybromobenzene showed $88\%$ yield of the Suzuki–Miyaura cross coupling product103 that indicates the excellent catalytic activity of this $\mathrm{Pd/MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst. The solid was used five times for the formation of biphenyl and very low concentration of Pd $(0.3~\mathrm{ppm})$ was observed in the solution by ICP-AES. The enhanced catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with respect to Pd/MIL-53(Al) shows the potential of MOFs to fine tuning their properties to adapt to the optimal conditions. Further work following this lead should be expected exploiting this potential.
The same group recently reported palladium NPs supported on amine-functionalized, mixed-linker metal–organic frameworks (MIXMOFs) based on MIL-53(Al) using the ion-exchange method.104 The mean diameter of Pd NPs is approximately $3.2~\mathrm{nm}$ . It was found that the Pd NPs supported on aminefunctionalized MIXMOFs are stable at high temperature and exhibited efficient catalytic activity for Heck reaction and can be easily recovered and reused.104
Another type of $\mathrm{C-}C$ coupling is the Sonogashira reaction of an aryl halide with a terminal alkyne to form the aryl substituted alkyne. It has been reported that this reaction can be promoted by Pd NPs included in MOF-5. Palladium NPs embedded on MOF- $5^{100}$ were prepared by a chemical deposition method. Powder XRD and SEM images of the resulting Pd/MOF-5 revealed the existence of crystal structure after loading of Pd NPs. On the other hand, TEM measurements showed the particle size of palladium ranging from 3 to $6\mathrm{nm}$ Obviously, this particle size is much too large to fit inside MOF-5 cavities. Pd/MOF-5 was used as catalyst for model Sonogashira coupling reaction between iodobenzene and phenylacetylene (Scheme 6).100
Out of the various parameters tested such as solvent, nature of base and reaction temperature, the best conditions were methanol with 1.5 equivalents of ${\mathrm{K}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{4}{\cdot}3{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ giving $98\%$ yield of diphenylacetylene in $3\mathrm{h.\Pd/MOF{-}}5$ has also shown good activity for many derivatives of iodobenzene and phenylacetylene, having various kinds of substituents giving the corresponding diarylethynes in good yields $(>90\%)$ . In addition, Pd/MOF-5 exhibited high activity when the reaction was performed with aliphatic alkynes and aryl iodide. However, Pd/MOF-5 as a solid catalyst possesses some limitations. In particular, ortho-substituted iodobenzenes showed lower yield than the para isomer. Furthermore, changing the aryl halide to bromo or chloro resulted in no conversion. Concerning Pd/MOF-5 stability, heterogeneity tests showed $2.1\mathrm{ppm}$ of Pd in the liquid phase after the reaction while the reusability data indicate that the catalyst loses its activity in the third use as evidenced by XPS studies. This deactivation was due to the oxidation of Pd from zero to $+2$ , an oxidation state that was absent in the fresh catalyst. In addition, TEM images showed agglomeration of Pd NPs.100
An analogous $\mathrm{Pd/MOF{-}}5^{101}$ sample has been tested for the aminocarbonylation of aryl iodides at atmospheric pressure of CO (Scheme 7).101 SEM images of MOF-5 and Pd/MOF-5 samples indicate no significant change in surface morphology after Pd incorporation. Hence, Pd NPs were well dispersed on the surface of MOF-5 and no obvious aggregation was observed for $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ at low levels of palladium loading (0.25 and $1.0\ \mathrm{wt\%}\$ ). On the other hand, aggregation of Pd NPs was observed for palladium loading higher than $1\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ . HRTEM images showed that Pd NPs on the MOF-5 matrix have particle size ranging between 3 and $12\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The reaction was screened with different parameters such as catalyst support, temperature, nature of base, reaction time and amount of catalyst. Quantitative isolated yield of morpholino( $\dot{p}$ -tolyl)methanone was achieved by using $15\mathrm{mg}$ of $0.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd/MOF-5 catalyst at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{h}$ at atmospheric pressure of CO. Sterically demanding ortho substituted substrates such as $o$ -iodotoluene required higher catalyst loading and prolonged reaction time to obtain $72\%$ yield. Aromatic primary amines showed lower reactivity and required higher catalyst loading of $1\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ and an extended reaction time of $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Unsubstituted aniline gave $85\%$ yield while lower yield $(74\%)$ was observed with $p$ -methylaniline. Sterically bulky aniline such as 2,6-dimethylaniline gave a promising yield of $53\%$ . The $0.5\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd-MOF-5 catalyst was used up to four recycles at 2 atm of CO without any appreciable change in the yield of morpholino( $\dot{p}$ -tolyl)methanone at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Pd leaching was negligible $(<2\ \mathrm{ppm}$ in each recycle) and no aminocarbonylation activity was observed using the supernatant liquid after the isolation of the solid catalyst.
The high performance of $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ allowed the use of this material as catalyst for the synthesis of moclobemide, an antidepressant drug, achieving $94\%$ yield under the standard conditions. Furthermore, the process was successfully scaled up to $\mathrm{1~mmol}$ of 4-chloroiodobenzene achieving $94\%$ yield as shown in Scheme 8.
Pd NPs were embedded inside the pores of MOF-5 by chemical vapour deposition using $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{5})(\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5})$ as a precursor, followed by hydrogenolysis at room temperature to obtain $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ with Pd NPs size ranging between 2 and $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ .102 The crystal structure was retained even with higher loading $(4.2\mathrm{wt\%})$ ) of Pd NPs inside MOF-5 as evidenced from powder XRD. The catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ was tested for Suzuki coupling reaction.102 Phenylboronic acid and bromobenzene were selected as substrates and high conversion, $81\%$ , was observed with $4.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Pd as catalyst in DMF using potassium carbonate as base. Decreasing Pd content to $0.17~\mathrm{wt\%}$ resulted in decrease of conversion to $7\%$ . However, when the catalyst with $4.2\mathrm{wt\%}$ Pd NPs loading was used for a second, third and fourth, the conversion decreased to 38, 29 and $21\%$ , respectively. Although, no particle aggregation was found from TEM analysis, powder XRD showed the disappearance of characteristic reflections of MOF-5 after first run. However, some additional information about the catalyst stability could have been obtained in the reusability experiments by determining Pd leaching.
Coupling reactions promoted by Pd NPs are widely used in organic synthesis for the preparation of pharmaceutical drugs.125,137 For this purpose, solid catalysts have the advantage of allowing an easier purification of the reaction products and the removal of Pd below the traces required for human consumption. Considering the successful examples of MOFs as hosts for Pd NPs, it would be of interest to apply these types of MOFs based Pd catalysts in this area where they can offer higher activity with low Pd content.
## 2.4 Indole functionalization/synthesis
From a sustainable chemistry viewpoint, direct transformation of a $\mathrm{C-H}$ bond into a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-C}}$ bond is the most efficient, and possibly the most environmentally friendly way for the functionalization of indoles. Recently, some studies have focused on the palladium-catalyzed direct C(2)–H arylation of indoles with activated arenes.138,139 However, the expensive homogeneous palladium catalysts cannot be recovered from the reaction mixture and, moreover, they are difficult to separate from the products at the purity level required for many drugs. In addition, the ${\mathrm{C}}(2){\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{H}}$ arylation of $N.$ -methylindoles catalyzed by ${\mathrm{Pd}}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}$ in solution is sensitive to the reaction conditions, such as solvent and/or base. For these reasons, it is of interest to test the activity of solid catalysts for this reaction.
In this context, Pd NPs $(0.5\mathrm{\wt\%})$ were embedded in a highly dispersed manner into the pores of MIL-101.111 The mean diameter of Pd NPs was $2.6\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Some of the NPs remain, however, outside the pores due to the electrostatic interaction between MIL-101 and the palladium ion.
$N$ -Methylindole and iodobenzene were employed as reagents to synthesize $N$ -methyl-2-phenylindole using Pd/MIL-101 as catalyst (Scheme 9). Out of the various parameters screened to achieve better yield of the desired product, the best conditions were those in which cesium acetate was used as base and dimethylformamide or dimethylacetamide as solvents at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The target substituted indole resulted in $85\%$ yield and no $\mathbf{C}_{3}$ arylation was observed.111 The reaction failed when only MIL-101 was used as a promoter or in the presence of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l0l}$ without cesium acetate. Increasing the amount of the catalyst in the range from 0.5 to $5\mathrm{mol\%}$ Pd led to a steady decrease (78 to $45\%$ ) of the yield of the desired product accompanied by a concomitant increase up to $32\%$ in the yield of the biphenyl byproduct. A commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ catalyst 1 $5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Pd) only gave traces of the $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ -arylation product and $22\%$ of the biphenyl byproduct. A low activity $25\%$ conversion was also observed for palladium NPs embedded on the $\mathrm{MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ catalyst.111 This comparison of the activity of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL}{\cdot}101\$ with that of other related or common Pd catalysts for the indole coupling with iodobenzene constitutes other remarkable example of the superior performance that Pd NPs can exhibit when encapsulated inside certain stable MOFs. The higher activity of Pd/MIL-101 may be explained as arising from the larger surface area and accessible mesoporous cages of MIL-101, which would be responsible for the high dispersion of the palladium active sites and facilitate the diffusion of the reactants into the pores reaching the active sites. Other alternative explanations such as that the enhanced surface Lewis acidity of MIL-101 may favor the adsorption of reagents, thus increasing the activity of this solid, cannot be however excluded.
Although the reaction was carried out in a highly polar solvent in the presence of base, leaching of Pd was observed to be $0.4\ \mathrm{ppm}$ from ICP-AES analysis. After using the catalyst five times, the crystallinity and particle size were retained as evidenced by XRD and TEM.
Due to the higher bond energy of the C–Br and C–Cl bonds with respect to $\mathrm{C-I}$ , the activation of bromobenzene and chlorobenzene is obviously more difficult. Therefore, the reaction of bromobenzene or chlorobenzene with $N.$ -methylindole required harsh conditions and extended reaction time. However, even at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ after $48\mathrm{{h}}$ , low yields were obtained for the reaction of bromobenzene $(31\%)$ and chlorobenzene $(21\%)$ with $N.$ -methylindole.111 The sterically hindered 2-iodoanisole, bearing an electron-donating group, also proceeded in a limited extent, affording the corresponding $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ -arylation product in moderate yield $(49\%)$ .111 These unsatisfactory yields for bromo and chloro arenes as well as for the ortho isomers clearly indicate that there is still room for new generations of more active heterogeneous Pd catalysts following the lead of highly active Pd complexes.
Recently, one pot synthesis of 2-arylindole from 2-iodoaniline and phenylacetylene using Pd/MIL-101 as catalyst in aqueous media was reported (Scheme 10).36 This result was compared with Pd/MCM-41. Pd/MIL-101 and $\mathrm{Pd/MCM{-}}41\$ were prepared with very similar palladium loading in 2.9 and $2.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ with a mean particle size of 2.6 and $3.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. It is relevant to mention that the structural morphology of MIL-101 allows the palladium NPs to be highly dispersed with the particle size ranging from 1 to $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , while in the case of mesoporous MCM-41 silica having straight channels, there was no remarkable confinement effect. As a result, a portion of the formed Pd particles might migrate out the pore channels of MCM-41, leading to agglomeration of Pd on the outer surface forming bigger particles. Despite similar Pd loading, $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ was much more active than Pd/MCM-41 and a complete conversion of 2-iodoaniline was achieved within $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ using Pd/MIL-101 while $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ was needed over Pd/MCM-41. This higher activity has been mainly attributed to the larger number of Pd active sites in Pd/MIL-101 relative to Pd/MCM-41 which would allow at the same Pd loading the operation of a higher number of cycles. Thus, the initial reaction rate over Pd/MIL-101 was 2 times that over Pd/MCM-41.36 It has also been proposed that part of the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ could derive from the enhanced surface hydrophobicity owing to the organic linkers BDC and the existence of Lewis acidity on MIL-101 at the exchangeable metal coordination sites $(\mathrm{Cr}^{3+})$ . Both factors would favor the adsorption and reactivity of 2-iodoaniline, and thus would contribute positively to the activity of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ . Further, commercial $3\%\ \mathrm{Pd/C}$ also showed lower reactivity than Pd/MIL-101 and this higher activity was ascribed to the higher dispersion of Pd active sites originated by the confinement effect of the Pd NPs on the cages of MIL-101. No oxidation of Pd was observed during the course of reaction and the leaching test showed the presence of negligible $0.9\mathrm{\ppb}$ concentration of Pd in the reaction mixture.
Besides the activity of fresh materials, catalyst stability was another issue addressed. Pd/MIL-101 could be reused ten times for the reaction between 2-iodoaniline and phenylacetylene without much loss in the catalytic activity, while Pd/MCM-41 showed a decrease in the yield of 2-phenylindole after the 6th run (Fig. 7).36 Based on TEM images, it was proposed that the deactivation of $\mathrm{Pd/MCM-41}$ could be mainly due to the collapse of the pore structure of the MCM-41 support and agglomeration of Pd NPs during the reaction. Apparently, the crystal structure of the MIL-101 matrix is more robust to hydrothermal treatment than mesoporous MCM-41 silica and Pd NPs remain confined in the cages of MIL-101, being the catalyst durable during the reaction. Therefore, the higher catalytic stability of Pd/MIL-101 should be attributed to the structure stability of MIL-101 which impedes the agglomeration of Pd NPs during the reaction. However, the reaction protocol to achieve high yields of the 2-arylindole uses triphenylphosphine, copper iodide and potassium carbonate as base that are impossible to separate from the solid catalyst afterwards, making contamination of Pd/MIL-101 unavoidable under these conditions. Alternative procedures would be convenient to increase even further the productivity of $\mathrm{Pd/MIL{-}l01}$ . Overall, the results of the two-component coupling of 2-iodoaniline and phenylacetylene exemplify again the superiority of Pd/MIL-101 over other conventional Pd catalysts, both in terms of activity and durability. Further work should be carried out to provide evidence to understand the origin of this higher activity and to exploit it to other related reactions. Additionally, the use of more robust MOFs or MOFs with optimal pore size/acidity could enhance the activity of Pd NPs even more.
## 3. Au NPs/MOFs
Catalysis by Au NPs has emerged as a hot topic bridging homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts.34 When the particle size is smaller than $20~\mathrm{nm}$ , Au NPs can exhibit a remarkable activity140 for many oxidations, reductions and coupling reactions that cannot be achieved with other noble metals. Thus, it has been found that Au NPs can affect the low-temperature CO oxidation141–143 much more efficiently and selectively than other noble metals.
Compared to the case of Pd, the catalytic activity of Au NPs is much more strongly dependent on the particle size and, therefore, a key issue in gold catalysis is how to prepare and maintain the size distribution in the appropriate range. One possibility to keep the particle size distribution constant would be to entrap the NP inside structurally robust MOFs. This section is focused on this topic.
## 3.1 Oxidation of alcohol
Selective oxidation of primary alcohols to aldehydes is a highly relevant transformation in organic chemistry due to the properties and chemical reactivity of these carbonylic compounds.144–146 Developing a suitable catalyst for this oxidation is a long standing research area. One of the most active catalysts for this transformation is Au NPs supported on suitable high surface area solids.147,148
In particular, Au supported on active carbon has shown high catalytic activity for the aerobic oxidation of alcohols under strong basic conditions.149 In the absence of base and solvent ceria nanoparticles of about $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ diameter have shown to be the support of choice.150 Other solids that had been used as supports of Au NPs include titania in the anatase phase,151 zirconia,152 iron oxides142 among others. These supports however do not provide a confinement of Au NPs and the use of zeolites and other micro or mesoporous hosts for Au NPs has been considerably less studied.
Recently, Haruta and co-workers have incorporated Au clusters on several MOFs such as MOF-5, MIL-53(Al), and $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ [BTC: 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate] using a volatile organogold complex, $\mathbf{Me}_{2}\mathbf{Au}(\mathrm{acac})$ , as a precursor.25 The procedure for incorporation of gold is very simple and consists of grinding the MOF and the gold complex to allow diffusion of the solid complex, followed by subsequent reduction and the formation of NPs.
It is relevant to mention that the size of the incorporated gold NPs is strongly dependent on the kind of MOFs, and its pore structure and size. Among the various MOFs used to incorporate gold, MIL-53(Al) showed the smallest particle size below $1\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
These gold containing catalysts were tested for the liquid phase aerobic oxidation in methanol of benzyl alcohol and 1-phenylethanol, two common test reactions for aerobic oxidation of alcohols.25 The use of $\mathbf{Au/MOF{-}}5$ as catalyst in the presence of base leads to $82\%$ conversion of benzyl alcohol resulting in the formation of $66\%$ of methyl benzoate and $3\%$ benzaldehyde in $^\mathrm{~1~h~}$ , while in the absence of base, $69\%$ conversion with 23% methyl benzoate and 31% benzaldehyde.25 Similarly, $\mathrm{Au/MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al})$ in the presence of base promoted the aerobic oxidation of benzyl alcohol with $98\%$ conversion in $23\mathrm{h}$ and a selectivity of $2\%$ of benzaldehyde and $77\%$ of methyl benzoate (Scheme 11). It has to be kept in mind that an equimolar amount of base is highly essential to promote the transformation with very high activity. However, the presence of base can cause the destruction of MOF crystal structure during the oxidation reaction.
Gold NPs incorporated on MOF-5 $(0.5~\mathrm{wt\%}$ Au with a mean diameter of $4.8\pm2.2\mathrm{nm}$ ) exhibited the highest catalytic activity for the 1-phenylethanol oxidation in the presence of base among all the materials containing Au embedded on MOFs prepared by solid grinding affording $79\%$ yield of acetophenone.25
However, tests on the leaching of gold during the oxidation reaction and study of the stability of the catalyst under the reaction conditions would make more realistic the comparison of the catalytic performance of MOF-5 embedded gold with other gold catalysts. As commented earlier, due to the low structural stability of MOF-5 to water, and other solvents, the issue of integrity of the catalyst must be always addressed for any catalyst based on MOF-5.
The loading and stabilization of very small, naked metal particles inside the cavities of zeolite imidazolate frameworks namely ZIF-8, and ZIF-90 $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{ICA})_{2}$ ; $\mathrm{ICA}~=$ imidazolate2-carboxyaldehyde) as host matrices have been reported in order to gain control on the dispersion of embedded gold NPs.115 The metal loading was obtained via gas phase infiltration of $[\mathrm{Au(CO)Cl]}$ followed by a thermal hydrogenation step to form the Au NPs. Homogeneous distribution of Au NPs throughout the ZIF matrix was observed with particle size ranging from 1 to $5\mathrm{nm}$ . ZIF-8 and ZIF-90 showed no activity in the oxidation of benzyl alcohol while Au/ZIF-8 and $\mathrm{{Au/ZIF{-}90}}$ exhibited $81\%$ and $13\%$ conversion with the selectivity towards methyl benzoate of $98\%$ and $50\%$ , respectively, in methanol medium (Scheme 12).115 Also, a small amount of benzaldehyde was observed and no formation of benzaldehyde dimethylacetal noticed.
Recently, MOF-5 and the metal oxide containing materials $Z_{\mathrm{nO/MOF}-5}$ and ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}/{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}5$ were loaded in a second step with the precursor $\scriptstyle{[\mathrm{ClAuCO}]}$ to yield intermediate materials namely $[\mathrm{ClAuCO}]/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ , $[\mathrm{ClAuCO}]/\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{MOF}.5\$ and $[\mathrm{ClAuCO}]/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5.$ 112 These composites were decomposed to Au/MOF-5, $\mathrm{Au}/Z\mathrm{nO}\ @\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ and $\mathbf{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathbf{MOF}{-}5$ under hydrogen at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It is very interesting to note that metal and metal oxide particles are not separated in different regions of the crystalline MOF matrix but are both present in one individual crystal.112 Gold NPs were distributed over the MOF matrix in a homogeneous fashion with a size distribution ranging between 1 and $3\mathrm{nm}$ . These catalysts were used for the liquid phase oxidation of benzyl alcohol. None of these catalysts showed positive activity in the absence of potassium carbonate while in the presence of base, $\mathbf{Au/MOF{-}}5$ showed $50\%$ conversion of benzyl alcohol to $48\%$ yield of methyl benzoate in $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The presence of metal oxide resulted in the increase of benzyl alcohol conversion. For example, $\mathbf{Au}/\mathbf{ZnO}\boldsymbol{@}\mathbf{MOF-5}$ and $\mathbf{Au}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}@\mathbf{MOF}-5$ gave $66\%$ and $72\%$ of methyl benzoate in the presence of potassium carbonate.112 Only trace amounts of benzaldehyde were observed and no formation of benzoic acid noticed.
Gold NPs have been incorporated also on MIL-101 using different methods and it was found that Au deposited by colloidal deposition with polyvinylpyrrolidine (PVP) as a protecting agent was the sample exhibiting the highest catalytic activity for aerobic oxidation of alcohols to aldehydes under base-free conditions.114 This higher activity may be due to lower mean diameter of gold NPs $(2.3~\mathrm{nm})$ compared to other methods of MIL-101 loading. However, how diffusion of preformed gold NPs stabilized by PVP occurs inside MOF is a matter that deserves further study to be rationalized. The performance of $\mathrm{\Au/MIL{-}l01}$ is remarkably good. Benzylic alcohols were oxidized to their corresponding aldehydes in high conversion $(>95\%)$ and selectivity $(>99\%)$ .114 Secondary aliphatic alcohols showed higher activity than primary counterparts. Alcohols with heteroatoms were also smoothly oxidized with high conversion and selectivity. The catalyst showed no leaching of gold and TEM analysis confirmed that particle size of gold before and after the oxidation reaction remains unaltered. The catalyst showed consistent yield $(>99\%)$ for the oxidation of 4-methoxybenzyl alcohol until the sixth cycle. This catalytic system showed very high TOF $(29300\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ for the aerobic oxidation of 1-phenylethanol $(35~\mathrm{mmol})$ at gold concentration $(4\times10^{-4}\mathrm{mol\%})$ in 0.5 h (Scheme 13).114
This notable catalytic activity is probably due to the high dispersion of Au NPs in combination with a beneficial synergistic effect of the MIL-101 support. As it is well-known,114 the support may play an important role, either direct or indirect, in determining the activity and selectivity of Au NPs. Certainly, the higher catalytic activity of this gold-containing MOF arises from the good chemical resistance to water and organic solvents of MIL-101 and the large pore size of MIL-101 (ca. 30 A˚ ) facilitating mass transfer of reactant/product molecules inside the pores. The high performance of MIL-101 as a host of gold NPs for aerobic oxidation is in line with the previously commented results obtained using Pd NPs using this MOF as a host.
## 3.2 Reduction of 4-nitrophenol
Recently, Au–Ag core–shell NPs have been deposited on ZIF-8 with $2\mathrm{-}6\mathrm{nm}$ without surfactant assistance by a sequential deposition–reduction procedure.116 As indicated in Fig. 8, two alternative approaches differing in the order in which the metal salts are reduced were used. $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Au}}(\mathcal{Q}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Ag}}$ core–shell NPs on ZIF-8 were prepared at different weight percentage of Au and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ with respect to ZIF-8. Bimetallic Au and $\mathbf{Ag}\mathbf{NPs}$ embedded on ZIF-8 were tested for the catalytic reduction of 4-nitrophenol to 4-aminophenol by $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ in water. The reaction did not occur over a $\operatorname{Au}\ @Z\operatorname{IF}-8$ $(2\mathrm{wt\%})$ catalyst and proceeds slowly with ‘‘induction period’’ over a $\mathrm{Ag}@\mathbb{Z}\mathrm{IF}{-}8$ $(2\ \mathrm{wt\%})$ catalyst. The catalytic activity was significantly improved by applying $\mathbf{Au-Ag}$ core–shell catalysts. Among all these catalysts, the one that was prepared following the approach II in Fig. 8 at $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Au and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ has the highest activity with an estimated rate constant of $4.97\times10^{-3}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , which is superior to that of most Au- and Ag-based catalysts under ambient conditions.116 Moreover, the catalyst also showed outstanding recyclability performance, revealing the stability of the catalyst, which was also confirmed by the TEM images after the use of the material as catalyst. Unexpectedly, the activity of a related sample containing also $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ of Au and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ , but prepared according to the approach I in Fig. 8 greatly decreased after being subjected to oxidation and reduction treatments at elevated temperatures due to the formation of a true $\mathrm{\AuAg}$ alloy with a NP size of around $3{-}8~\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 3.3 One-pot synthesis of dibenzylamine
Gold NPs have been prepared with particle size smaller than $5\mathrm{nm}$ on MIL-53(Al), MOF-5 and CPL-2 $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(p z d c)_{2}(b p y)]}_{n}$ .113 In particular, $80\%$ of Au particles could be deposited as clusters with diameters below $1.6~\pm~1.0~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ over MIL-53(Al). The particle size of gold on MOF-5 and CPL-2 is $3.3\pm2.3$ and $2.4\pm1.0$ respectively. This series of gold containing catalysts was tested for the one pot synthesis of dibenzylamine (DBA) from benzylamine (B) by sequential oxidation/hydrogenation via the formation of $N.$ -benzylidenebenzylamine (I) as shown in Scheme 14. Although, $\mathrm{{Au/CPL-2}}$ and $\mathrm{Au/MOF{-}}5$ showed high conversion towards the oxidation of $\mathbf{B}$ to $\mathbf{I}$ , a trace amount of
DBA was observed.113 In contrast, $\mathrm{Au/MIL}{-}53(\mathrm{Al})$ exhibited the highest conversion in the oxidation step and also in the hydrogenation of I to give DBA in $91\%$ yield. This clearly indicates the high activity of gold deposited on MIL-53(Al) due to its smaller particle size and uniform distribution. A comparative study with $\mathrm{\Au/TiO}_{2}$ (average Au particle size $2.9\pm0.5\ \mathrm{nm},$ as catalyst shows that product DBA was not formed and resulted in only $43\%$ yield of I. These catalytic data can be understood considering the role that particle size and support play in gold catalysis and shows clearly the beneficial advantage that MOFs as porous matrices can have over other supports.
## 4. Ru NPs/MOFs
Ruthenium NPs can be easily obtained by reduction of $\mathrm{Ru}^{3+}$ salts and then they are stable under reductive conditions. For this reason, Ru NPs can be employed as hydrogenation catalysts for which they enjoy a good balance between price and affordability, stability and catalytic activity.
A simple and efficient route for the immobilization of Ru NPs onto La(BTC) $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ MOF using ${\mathrm{RuCl}}_{3}{\cdot}3{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ as a precursor in supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ –methanol solution at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ has been reported.31 The average particle size was about $2\:\mathrm{nm}$ for a loading of $0.98\mathrm{\wt\%}$ . This catalyst was used for the hydrogenation of cyclohexene and benzene with hydrogen (Scheme 15). Cyclohexene could be converted almost completely into cyclohexane within $0.5\mathrm{h}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure of $2\ \mathrm{MPa}$ at a substrate $\mathrm{\bf{R}u}$ molar ratio of 5000, reaching a turnover frequency (TOF) value around $10000~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . In a similar way, hydrogenation of benzene to cyclohexane catalyzed by this ${\mathrm{Ru/MOF}}$ was completed in $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and with a $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ pressure of $6\ \mathrm{MPa}$ at the substrate/Ru molar ratio of 5000 with a TOF value around 2475.31
In order to determine the relative efficiency of Ru embedded in MOF, benzene hydrogenation was performed under the same conditions with commercial $\mathrm{Ru/C}$ , whereby a TOF value of 664 was measured. Again, comparison of the activity of $\mathrm{Ru/MOF}$ with that of the most common active carbon supported Ru shows the superior performance that can be achieved by entrapment of the MNPs inside MOFs.
The stability of the $\mathrm{Ru/MOF}$ catalyst was investigated for the cyclohexene hydrogenation. For this reaction, the catalyst could be reused five times with almost no activity loss $(\mathrm{TOF}~>~9900)$ ), indicating that $\mathrm{Ru/MOF}$ was rather stable under the reaction conditions.31 Further, the morphology of the $\mathrm{Ru/MOF}$ composite after five reuses by TEM was compared with that of the fresh $\mathrm{Ru/MOF}$ sample without observing agglomeration of the MNPs after consecutive catalytic tests.
Ru embedded on the La(BTC) $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ catalyst was also prepared in aqueous solution instead of supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ but the resulting sample had a much lower activity (TOF 450) for benzene hydrogenation. This lower activity may be explained considering the presence of larger Ru particles for the $\mathrm{Ru/MOF}$ sample prepared in water. This control experiment indicates that supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ –methanol solution could be superior to conventional liquid solvents for preparing metal embedded MOF catalysts and opens the way for the deposition of other NPs on other MOFs exploiting this underdeveloped methodology.
The gas-phase loading of MOF-5 with the volatile compound $[\mathrm{Ru(cod)(cot)}]$ was followed by solid-state magic angle spinning $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectroscopy.117 Subsequently, the adsorbed complex inside the porous structure of MOF-5 was submitted to the hydrogenolysis at 3 bar and $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ resulting in the formation of $\mathrm{{RuNPs}}$ in a typical size range of $1.5\mathrm{-}1.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The inclusion of the Ru NPs inside the intact MOF-5 matrix was confirmed by TEM, selected area electron diffraction, powder XRD and X-ray absorption spectroscopy.117 The loading of $\mathtt{R u}$ on MOF-5 was as high as $30\mathrm{\mt{\%}}$ (Fig. 9). In contrast, room temperature hydrogenolysis of the $\mathbf{Ru(cod)(cot)]}_{3.5}/\mathbf{MOF}{-5}$ inclusion compound does not lead to the quantitative formation of Ru NPs.
$\mathrm{Ru}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ was tested for the hydrogenation of benzene and for benzyl alcohol oxidation. In the case of hydrogenation of benzene, the reaction was carried out at 3 bar $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $75^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $20\mathrm{~h~}$ , reaching a conversion of benzene to cyclohexane around $25\%$ . This performance of $\mathrm{Ru}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ seems to be lower than that previously commented for the $\mathrm{Ru/MOF}$ in which La(BTC) was used as a host. The powder XRD of $\mathrm{Ru/MOF{-}}5$ after its use as catalyst revealed, however, that the MOF-5 structure as well as the typical broad reflection at $44.0^{\circ}$ for Ru was retained.
It is well documented in the literature that $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ embedded on different matrices could be used as heterogeneous catalysts for alcohol oxidation.153,154 In this context, $\mathrm{Ru}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ material can be easily converted to ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{x}/{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}5$ by oxidation with diluted $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ gas. The catalytic activity of ${\mathrm{RuO}}_{x}/{\mathrm{MOF}}{-5}$ was tested for the oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde in toluene with oxygen at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , reaching a conversion of $25\%$ in $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . These values of activity are not very exciting according to the state of the art that can achieve complete alcohol oxidation, but they typically use expensive noble metals. Examination of the powder XRD for the material after the oxidation reaction revealed a breakdown of the MOF-5 structure and a loss of its high porosity as well. This lack of structural stability may be attributed to the fact that a stoichiometric release of water takes place during this oxidation reaction. MOF-5 is well known to be rather water-sensitive even for adsorbed water percentages as low as 4–6 wt%.155
## 5. Cu NPs/MOFs
Loading of copper, palladium and gold NPs onto the matrix of MOF- $5^{118}$ was performed using organometallic precursors $[(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{5}{-}\mathbf{C}_{5}\mathbf{H}_{5})\mathbf{C}\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{P}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{e}_{3})]$ , $[(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{5}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}_{5}\mathbf{H}_{5})\mathbf{P}\mathrm{d}(\boldsymbol{\eta}^{3}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}_{3}\mathbf{H}_{5})]$ and $[(\mathrm{CH}_{3})\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{PMe}_{3})].$ , whereby resulting NPs had an average particle size of 3–4, 1.4 and $5{-}20~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. A similar procedure by gas-phase infiltration with organometallic precursors was performed to obtain a MOF-5 loaded with $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ and ZnO NPs.156
The purpose was to test the catalytic activity of these materials for methanol synthesis from the syn gas mixture. $\mathrm{Cu/MOF{-}}5$ exhibited better catalytic efficiency than Pd and Au over MOF-5 with the production of methanol at a rate of 70, 47.3 and $0~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}_{\mathrm{MeOH}}~\mathbf{g}^{-1}{}_{\mathrm{cat}}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{cu}$ , Pd and Au on MOF-5, respectively.118 This better efficiency of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ was compared with a recently reported novel embedded mesoporous $\mathrm{Cu}/Z\mathrm{nO}\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{MCM}{-41}$ catalyst prepared by metal– organic chemical vapour deposition with a production rate of $5.8\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}_{\mathrm{MeOH}}\ \mathrm{m}_{\mathrm{Cu}}^{2}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ .157 This activity of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ is surprising as the promotion of Cu by Zn or ZnOx species158 is generally considered essential for observation of the catalytic activity for methanol synthesis. Apparently this is not necessary in the case of MOF-5, or it can be considered that the promotional effect of $Z\mathrm{n}$ to introduce activity in copper is provided in a novel way by the $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}}}$ clusters present on the MOF-5 framework. In addition, the stability of the $\mathrm{cu}$ particles and MOF-5 lattice not collapsing under the catalysis conditions $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{H}_{2})$ is remarkable.
## 6. Pt NPs/MOFs
MOF-177 has been used as a porous matrix for loading of $43\mathrm{\ut{\%}}$ Pt NPs.119 Most of the Pt NPs were in the range of $2\mathrm{-}3~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with some of them in the range of $5\mathrm{nm}$ in size due to agglomeration. $\mathrm{Pt/MOF{-}177}$ was used for hydrogen storage and as catalyst for aerobic oxidation of benzylic and allylic alcohols at room temperature under base and solvent free conditions (Scheme 16).119
Benzylic substrates showed very good reactivity with TON values higher than 500 and cinnamyl alcohol with 385. In contrast, aerobic oxidation of iodo and bromo substituted benzyl alcohols failed due to their steric hindrance that impedes these substrates to reach the active sites. This is, however, somewhat surprising considering that the kinetic diameter of bromobenzyl alcohol (isomer is not mentioned) is not that much larger than that of the parent benzyl alcohol. Aliphatic alcohols were found to be unreactive under identical conditions.
After using $\mathrm{Pt/MOF{-}177}$ as catalyst for the aerobic oxidation of benzyl alcohol, powder XRD of the used material revealed that breakdown of the MOF lattice had occurred leading to a negligible conversion of benzyl alcohol in an attempted reuse. This lack of MOF-177 stability could be anticipated from the fact that the $\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}O}$ cluster constituting some MOFs is highly water sensitive and water is produced as a byproduct during the oxidation. This result raises again the issue of structural stability under the reaction conditions as a prerequisite for the use of a given MOF as a host of MNPs to be used as a heterogeneous catalyst.
## 7. Ni NPs/MOFs
Crystalline mesoporous MOF (MPMOF-1) has a MTN-type framework comprised of 68 super-tetrahedral building units $\mathrm{Tb_{16}(T A T B)_{16}}$ in a unit cell. Two meso-cages of 3.9 and $4.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter are connected through pentagonal and hexagonal small windows with a diameter of 1.5 and $1.7\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively.159
Due to the robust and large super-tetrahedral building units, each spherical meso-cage is isolated from the others. This is in sharp contrast to conventional mesoporous catalyst supports, such as SBA-15, because their pore system is cylindrical and pore sizes are typically $7-10~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Three different Ni/MPMOF- $\cdot1^{121}$ samples were prepared differing in the precursor loading with nickel content 20, 30 and $35\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively. The determination of particle size from TEM studies was not firmly conclusive but supports the dispersibility of the Ni particles in MPMOF-1. As in the case of $\mathtt{R u}$ NPs and other transition metals, Ni MNPs do not undergo oxidation for those reactions in which hydrogen or other reducing agents are employed. In the present case, Ni/MPMOF-1 was employed as a hydrogenation catalyst.
Styrene to ethylbenzene was chosen as a model reaction to check the catalytic activity of Ni/MPMOF-1 $(20\ \mathrm{\wt\%}$ Ni loading) and the reaction was completed within $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ under $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in methanol at room temperature. This result indicates that the Ni NPs do not prevent the diffusion of styrene and ethylbenzene into the pores.
Hydrogenation of nitrobenzene using Ni/MPMOF-1 failed under the same reaction conditions used for the conversion of styrene to ethylbenzene. However, reduction of nitrobenzene could be promoted by $\mathrm{Ni}/$ MPMOF-1 containing 30 or $35\mathrm{wt\%}$ Ni being completed within $15\mathrm{min}$ when $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ was employed as a hydrogen source in methanol. Formation of aniline was promoted by the same Ni/MPMOF-1 sample even after three reaction cycles, indicating the framework stability. Leaching of the Ni NPs was checked by stirring Ni/MPMOF-1 in MeOH for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and using the filtrate for a control experiment for formation of aniline from nitrobenzene and $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ whereby no product was detected. It was also concluded that the reduction of benzene derivatives is highly influenced by Ni NPs size, as well as the pore space available for the diffusion of the reactants and the pore environment.
Recently, Ni NPs at $7.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ were supported on MOF- $5^{120}$ by the simple wet impregnation procedure resulting in the particle size between 2 and $6\mathrm{nm}$ . This catalyst was tested for the hydrogenation of crotonaldehyde to give crotyl alcohol or butyraldehyde as products. The yield of butyraldehyde was only $0.14\%$ when the reaction was conducted in the presence of MOF-5 at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , while with $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ , the conversion of crotonaldehyde increased to $91\%$ with high selectivity of butyraldehyde $(98.30\%)$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40~\mathrm{min}$ . Moreover, crotonaldehyde conversion increased to $99.45\%$ with a slight decrease of selectivity $(90.11\%)$ at $60\ \mathrm{min}$ . In contrast, $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ $28\mathrm{\wt{\%}}$ showed $83.06\%$ crotonaldehyde with $94.38\%$ selectivity of butyraldehyde at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $40~\mathrm{min}$ . $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ exhibited higher TOF compared to $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ with the value of 154.6 and $73.9\ \mathrm{~h~}^{-1}$ , respectively. Although $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{MOF}{-}5$ was reused three times with slight changes in the conversion and selectivity, no leaching data were reported.
## 8. Ag NPs/MOFs
The preparation of monodispersed silver NPs has attracted considerable attention because they exhibit a strong surface plasmon resonance that depends on the particle size and shape.160 However, control of the size and shape of silver particles is much more difficult than that of gold or platinum particles.161 MNPs are often prepared by embedding micro- or mesoporous inorganic materials with metal compounds or metal nanocomposites, followed by reduction processes.162 The method requires a high temperature and long reaction time and, therefore, formation of small monodispersed $(<5\mathrm{nm})$ silver particles is difficult. Silver NPs can also be prepared by reduction of a solution of $\mathrm{{Ag(I)}}$ ions with reducing agents in the presence of surfactants or polymers, but the method cannot completely prevent the formation of large aggregates of silver particles.163 Therefore, a reliable preparation procedure of monodispersed small silver NPs under mild conditions without extra reduction processes would be very useful. However, it is extremely difficult to find an appropriate solid support that can reduce $\mathrm{{Ag}(I)}$ ions to silver particles at ambient temperature.164
In this context, the preparation of the Ni based MOF using macrocyclic ligand to form $[\mathrm{(Ni(C_{10}H_{26}N_{6}))_{3}(b p d c)_{3}}]{\cdot2}\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{N}$ $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{\C_{10}H_{26}N_{6}}$ : 1,4,8,11-tetraazacyclotetradecane; bpdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate; ${\mathrm{C}}_{5}{\mathrm{H}}_{5}{\mathrm{N}} $ : pyridine) at roomtemperature and used as a solid matrix to synthesize small silver NPs with the size of $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ has been reported.165 This MOF was constructed by packing of the linear coordination polymer chains formed from the nickel macrocyclic complex $\mathrm{[Ni(C_{10}H_{26}N_{6})](C l O_{4})_{2}}$ and bpdc $\cdot^{2-}$ ions in a water–pyridine mixture (Scheme 17).
Attempts to prepare silver NPs using $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{cyclam})(\mathrm{bpydc})]$ · $5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (cyclam $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 1,4,8,11$ -tetraazacyclotetradecane; bpydc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl- ${\bar{5}},{\bar{5}}^{\prime}$ -dicarboxylate), which was assembled from $\mathrm{[Ni(cyclam)](ClO_{4})_{2}}$ and bpydc $\:2-\:$ ions in water166 failed due to the dissociation of $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{cyclam})]^{2+}$ ions in the presence of ${\bf A g N O}_{3}$ solution due to the strong affinity of bpydc $\mathrel{\mathop:}^{2-}$ towards $\mathrm{Ag(\mathrm{I})}$ ions.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy data also indicate that $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{0}$ and ${\mathrm{Ni}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ coexist in the solid. To see if the silver NPs can be formed only by the non-porous insoluble solid of the ${\bf N}_{1}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ macrocyclic complex, crystals of $\mathrm{[Ni(C_{10}H_{26}N_{6})](C l O_{4})_{2}}$ were immersed in a methanolic solution of $\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ for several hours. The reaction did not produce silver NPs, which indicates that both the redox-active ${\bf N}_{1}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ -macrocyclic component and the porous structure are necessary to obtain NPs.
Silver clusters have been deposited on $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ , MOF-508 (Fig. 10) $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{BDC})(\mathrm{bpy})_{0.5}]$ and MIL-68 (In) [indium(III)- terephthalate] as solid matrices to control the particle size using a solution infiltration technique with silver nitrate as a precursor in aqueous ethanolic solution to obtain $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ clusters around $1~\mathrm{nm}$ .167 During the loading of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ , the crystal structure was maintained as evidenced by powder XRD. Much larger particles $\cdot15\ \mathrm{nm})$ were observed using TEM due to the damage of the MOF crystal structure by the electron beam. Careful study of the effects of the TEM electron beam on these relatively soft materials shows that greater than pore-size clusters are most probably artifacts of the imaging technique. Thus, much care must always be taken in the interpretation of electron microscopic data on MOFs and other soft materials.167
The distinct advantage of MOFs acting as host matrices derive from the nanoscale dimensions of their channels or cavities, in which small monodispersed particles are formed and immobilized avoiding their aggregation into larger particles.118 In this context, a new Ni-MOF has been synthesized with $\mathrm{H_{2}D T D C}$ (dithieno $[3,2-b;2^{\prime},3^{\prime}$ -d]thiophene dicarboxylic acid) as a ligand comprising three thiophene rings. This Ni-MOF has been used as a support for the deposition of silver NPs with a size of $1\mathrm{nm}$ (Scheme 18) with a silver content of $7.03\mathrm{wt\%}$ as evidenced by XPS analysis.
$\mathbf{Ag/Ni-MOF}^{122}$ was used to catalyze the three-component coupling reaction of paraformaldehyde with diethylamine and phenylacetylene (Scheme 19). The optimized reaction conditions resulted in the corresponding product with a yield of $95\%$ . On the other hand, either aromatic aldehydes (benzaldehyde) or branched aliphatic aldehydes (isobutyraldehyde) hardly gave any reaction product, strongly suggesting the operation of shape selectivity to reaction substrates in Ni-MOF catalysis. For instance, when the mixture of formaldehyde and an aromatic aldehyde or a branched aliphatic aldehyde was added to the reaction system, only the corresponding product of formaldehyde was detected. It could be, however, that instead of steric hindrance the remarkably higher reactivity of formaldehyde compared to other carbonyl compounds is the factor responsible for this behavior. Further studies are necessary to address this issue. The presence of a bulky alkyl or aryl group on the alkyne moiety also affected negatively the reaction.
The catalyst was reused five times without any decrease in the yield of the desired product. However, there was no study about the catalyst stability as the present study uses strongly basic amine and the leaching of silver to the solution was not considered. Besides the reuse of the catalyst, characterization of spent catalyst by TEM, or checking of the stability of the crystal structure by powder XRD should have given valuable information.
In any case, the above result shows that it would be interesting to study in more detail the catalyst activity of Ag NPs embedded in various MOFs, particularly for epoxidation of alkenes and other reactions for which Ag NPs have found to be superior to other noble metals.
## 9. Concluding remarks and future prospects
Taking into account the novelty of MOFs and the recent interest in exploiting their properties as solid catalysts, there is no doubt that the field of catalysis by embedded MNPs in MOFs will continue to attract much interest in the recent years. The above described reports have mostly taken advantage of MOFs as passive hosts defining porosity and the reaction cavities. In this way, the metal particle size is mostly limited to the size of the cavities that is in the perfect nanometric range to obtain highly active MNPs. However, one aspect that has been clearly underdeveloped is the simultaneous combination of the passive role of MOFs as hosts embedding MNPs with an active role putting into use either the metal nodes or the organic linkers. In this way, active sites with different nature would be combined in a single material allowing cascade transformations in which several organic reactions are coupled and catalyzed by a single material. Obvious developments in this field will be to use oxidation or hydrogenation reactions coupled with an acid/base catalyzed process. Thus, for instance, the MNPs could promote hydrogenation of one functional group that will be later substituted or removed by the acid site.
As in any application based on MOFs, stability of the structure is a key issue. In this regard also for their use as hosts of MNPs, more stable MOFs that allow general reaction conditions in different solvents and moderate temperatures will be very important since productivity of MOF is related with their stability. In this context, it should be noted that some of the previously commented work has been carried out with very unstable MOFs such as MOF-5 and the results and activity could be easily improved if more robust materials are employed for the same processes.
Pd NPs embedded on MIL-101 as host matrices have been successfully employed as heterogeneous catalysts for C–C bond forming reactions, hydrogenations, condensation and indole synthesis. It is likely that, the scope of this catalyst will be expanded to other reactions such as oxidation of alcohol or hydrogenation reactions with very high productivity. It has to be pointed out that developing a good catalytic system for the aerobic oxidation of alcohols to the corresponding aldehydes with high selectivity and productivity is a challenging target using MOFs as catalysts. This oxidation often fails due to the poisoning of active sites by the carboxylic acid produced during the over oxidation of aldehyde. However, recently Au/MIL-101 exhibited a very high activity for the oxidation of 1-phenylethanol with high TOF and it would be important to expand the scope of this catalyst to other aerobic oxidations of alcohols.
Aerobic oxidation of olefins to their epoxides with high selectivity is another important reaction from an industrial point of view due to the ample use of these compounds in the polymer industry and as intermediates in organic synthesis. Another point of interest is to develop Cu NPs embedded on MOFs and to use them as heterogeneous catalysts as copper metal is cheap and can catalyze a wide range of organic reactions when it is present as NPs. Also, it is of interest to exploit other MNPs embedded on robust MOFs as heterogeneous catalysts for general organic reactions.
Another aspect that is still clearly underdeveloped is the possibility to use MOFs as matrices for multicomponent supramolecular systems. For instance, the combination of MNPs with different metals or metal NPs/metal oxide embedded in MOFs has not been exploited considering the diversity of materials and reactions that can be promoted. For instance, there are almost no reports on the preparation of metal alloys or core–shell MNPs in MOFs while from the data available in the literature it is clear that these NPs with two different metals have a large catalytic potential. Also, the combination of nanoparticulate metal–metal oxide is very powerful in catalysis as it has been demonstrated in the case of supported gold catalysts. Thus, the catalytic activity of gold is extremely dependent on the nature of the support. One case of particular relevance will be the combination of $\mathrm{\bfAu\mathrm{-}C e O}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{\Au{-}T i O}_{2}$ based materials. It is clear that examples of this type of catalysts embedded in MOFs will be highly relevant if their performance improves that obtained for known encapsulated solids.
Finally, also of interest will be to perform systematic studies in which the particle size of the MNPs is finely tuned in the range of the nanometer scales. The wide diversity of MOFs in terms of structure and pore dimensions offers the possibility to provide valid comparisons of catalytic activity of MNPs as a function of the particle size.
The above comments serve to illustrate a few of the many new opportunities that encapsulation of MNPs in MOFs offer for heterogeneous catalysis.
